        "source": "kg"
      }
    },
    {
      "node_id": "kg_doc_7",
      "node_type": "kg",
      "content": "The Beaver and Erie Canal, also known as the Erie Extension Canal, was part of the Pennsylvania Canal system and consisted of three sections: the Beaver Division, the Shenango Division, and the Conneaut Division. The canal ran 136 mi north–south near the western edge of the state from the Ohio River to Lake Erie through Beaver County, Lawrence County, Mercer County, Crawford County, and Erie County, Pennsylvania.",
      "depth": 0,
      "parent_id": null,
      "value": 0.9999999820498212,
      "temperature": 0.9999999820498212,
      "expanded": false,
      "visit_count": 0,
      "persistent": true,
      "score_breakdown": {},
      "metadata": {
        "title": "Beaver and Erie Canal",
        "raw_score": 4.004038508717967,
        "source": "kg"
      }
    },
    {
      "node_id": "kg_doc_6",
      "node_type": "kg",
      "content": "The 17th Street Canal is the largest and most important drainage canal in the city of New Orleans. Operating with Pump Station 6, It moves water into Lake Pontchartrain. The canal, along with the Orleans Canal and the London Avenue Canal, form the New Orleans Outfall Canals. The 17th Street Canal forms a significant portion of the boundary between the city of New Orleans and Metairie, Louisiana. The canal has also been known as the Metairie Outlet Canal and the Upperline Canal.",
      "depth": 0,
      "parent_id": null,
      "value": 0.9710575923814259,
      "temperature": 0.9710575923814259,
      "expanded": false,
